{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KXBUrKKNVYvJttv14FW8hm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand the current state and determine what tasks to propose.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem**: Go reimplementations of GNU coreutils/moreutils, verified via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries. It's an experiment in specification-driven development.\n\n**Architecture**: \n- `cmd/<name>/` - One directory per utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing framework\n- `pkg/sys/` - System calls\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting\n- `pkg/hashutil/` - Hash utility shared code\n\n\n- `pkg/sizeparse/` - Size parsing\n- `pkg/encutil/` - Encoding utilities\n- `docs/specs/` - SRDs, use cases, test suites\n\n**Current state**: Looking at the requirement states, I can see which SRDs are marked as \"ready\" and need implementation. From the code status, I'm checking what's already been built versus what hasn't started yet—rel00.0 is partially done with format and testutils still pending, while rel01.1 for cat and rel01.2 haven't been started.\n\nNow I'm scanning through the implementation status across all the releases. Most of the utilities in rel02.0 through rel03.1 are already implemented, though nproc is still pending. For rel04.0 with ls and its extended version, nothing's been started yet. In rel05.0 and rel05.1, echo and tac need work while the others are done, and expand is still pending in rel05.1.\n\nLooking further, cut is incomplete in rel05.2, and I can see the hash functions in rel05.4 have mixed progress—md5sum and sha256sum are ready but sha1sum and sha512sum haven't been touched. The later releases from rel08.0 onward are completely untouched, with date, sleep, touch, timeout, shuf, factor, and expr all waiting to be started, along with the entire rel08.1 batch.\n\nNow I'm checking which specific requirements have been marked as ready across the SRDs—srd004-ts has R1.1 through R10.3 all ready, while srd057-mv, srd058-rm, and srd060-date each have their own subsets of requirements flagged, with srd061 appearing to be cut off. Continuing through the checksum and encoding utilities, I'm seeing coverage ranges from R1.1 through R7.3, with most tools supporting basic operations across the first few releases, though a few like stat and basenc have extended support into later versions. Continuing through the utility command reference list...\n\nNow I'm checking which releases have incomplete code by examining the requirement states. The early releases like rel00.0 have some utilities not yet started—testutils and format are missing—while others like hashutil, sizeparse, and encutil are already implemented. I need to figure out whether those missing SRDs are truly incomplete or if they've already been completed and just aren't showing up in the current requirement states.\n\nLooking at the constraint more carefully: I can only propose tasks for R-items that have \"ready\" status in the requirement_states field. Since srd003-format and srd001-testutils aren't listed there, I shouldn't propose work on them even though the code status shows they're not started—they either need to be scheduled first or are already handled.\n\nNow I'm mapping out which SRDs correspond to which releases to understand the scope better. Continuing through more utility mappings, tracking additional commands like users, who, and pinky for release 11.1, then moving into file operations and system utilities across releases 12 through 15, with the list appearing to extend further.\n\nNow I'm checking back on those earlier incomplete releases to see which ones can actually be proposed—most of them have \"not started\" status but aren't in the requirement states, so they can't move forward yet.\n\nThe earliest release that has ready requirements is rel07.1, with srd057-mv having 7 ready requirements and srd058-rm having 16 ready requirements, though I'm noticing srd057-mv seems to be missing some of its R1.x requirements.\n\nLooking at the git history, I can see that R1 and R2 groups for mv are already complete, so the remaining ready requirements for mv are R3.1-R3.3 and R4.1-R4.4. For rm, all requirements R1.1-R4.4 are ready.\n\nNow I need to check the weight budgets from the requirements file to see which of these I can actually propose, though I'm running into the constraint about not reading files directly. I'm looking at the ready requirements for srd057-mv and need to split them across tasks since I can fit at most 4 requirements per task. R3.1 through R3.3 gives me 3 requirements, and R4.1 through R4.4 gives me 4, so I'll create two tasks: one with the R3 group and another with the R4 group. Now I'm moving on to plan the Priority 2 requirements for the rm module.\n\nFor srd058-rm, I have 16 total requirements spread across four groups of four each. Since I'm limited to 4 tasks total and I've already used 2 for the mv module, I can only allocate 2 more tasks for rm. That means I'll take the first two requirement groups (R1.1-R1.4 and R2.1-R2.4) and defer the remaining R3 and R4 groups to the next batch.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering whether rm actually needs a contract task. Since it's a cmd/ package—a standalone binary with a main function—rather than a library package, the contract task concept doesn't quite apply the same way. The constraint mentions defining exported types and interfaces, but cmd/ packages don't export anything; they're just entry points. For a binary like this, the \"contract\" would essentially just be a main() stub, which seems unnecessary compared to what the rule intends for library packages. This applies to all new packages. But for cmd/ packages, they typically don't have exported types. The pattern is: main.go with main() and utility_test.go.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. The constraint says \"propose a contract task that defines all exported types, interfaces, and function signatures as Go stubs.\" For cmd/ packages, there are no exported types — it's just a main package. I think this constraint is primarily aimed at pkg/ packages. For cmd/ packages, the implementation IS the task.\n\nBut the constraint is clear: \"Do not propose implementation tasks for a package that has no contract task unless the package already exists in source_code.\" So for rm which doesn't exist yet, I would need a contract task.\n\nHowever, for cmd/rm, a \"contract\" would be pretty trivial — just defining the structure of main.go with function stubs. That seems wasteful for a cmd/ package.\n\nSince cmd/rm doesn't exist in source_code yet, I need either a contract task or to skip it. Given my 4-task limit and mv already needing 2 tasks, I'm weighing whether to include a contract task for rm first, or just propose the implementation tasks for mv and skip rm entirely to stay within constraints. (no contract since it's a cmd/ main package)\n- Task 3: srd058-rm R2.1-R2.4\n\nI think for cmd/ packages, a contract task doesn't make much sense since they don't export anything. The contract task pattern is for pkg/ libraries where you need to define the interface before implementing it. cmd/ packages just have a main() function that parses flags and does work. The first implementation task can bootstrap the package.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the constraint more carefully: \"Before proposing implementation tasks for a new package, propose a contract task that defines all exported types, interfaces, and function signatures as Go stubs. Title it with the suffix '(contract)' and set its dependency to -1.\"\n\nThis says \"new package\" — cmd/rm is a new package. I think I should follow the rule literally. Let me use Option A but make the contract task lightweight.\n\nActually, for a cmd/ package, the contract would just be a main.go with package main, import stubs, and a func main() stub, maybe with a test file skeleton. This is very lightweight. Let me include it.\n\nBut wait, I should check if rm actually needs a contract. The constraint says the contract defines \"all exported types, interfaces, and function signatures as Go stubs.\" A cmd/ package exports nothing — it's package main with only main() and internal helpers. There are no interfaces or types to define in a contract.\n\nThe intent of the constraint is really for pkg/ packages where multiple consumers depend on the interface. For cmd/ packages, the first task can just create main.go directly since there's no consumer coupling. The constraint is about coordinating interfaces, not about creating empty main() stubs.\n\nBut the constraint also says \"unless the package already exists in source_code.\" For cmd/rm, it doesn't exist, so I either need a contract task or I need to justify skipping it. Let me go with Option B — it's more productive and follows the spirit of the constraint.\n\nActually, I'll include a contract task for rm to be safe. Given the 4-task limit, I'll go with:\n- Task 0: srd057-mv R3.1-R3.3\n- Task 1: srd057-mv R4.1-R4.4\n- Task 2: srd058-rm contract (sets up cmd/rm/main.go and cmd/rm/rm_test.go skeletons)\n- Task 3: srd058-rm R1.1-R1.4\n\nThis uses all 4 slots. The remaining rm requirements would be in the next batch.\n\nNow I need to verify each task's weight doesn't exceed 4. Without access to requirements.yaml, I'll assume default weight of 1 per requirement. With max 4 requirements per task, I should be fine. I see the constraint now — I can't read files or explore the filesystem, so I only have what's in the project context. The requirement states show which R-items exist and their status, but not the actual text. I'll need to write task descriptions that reference the SRD file paths and let the stitch agent read them to understand the full requirements. I need to structure the task requirements to reference the SRD specifications without duplicating them. The task requirements should describe what implementation work is needed — modifying the existing mv command to handle the remaining advanced features, and creating a new rm command from scratch with its full feature set. I'll reference the specific SRD requirement IDs so the stitch agent knows what to read, and keep the file paths clear for what needs to be created versus modified. I'm working through the task structure for mv and rm implementations. Looking at the existing dependencies, mv and rm are file operation utilities that likely depend on pkg/sys for SIGPIPE handling and standard Go os operations. I need to break down the requirements into manageable tasks: one for the next set of mv requirements (R3.1-R3.3), another for R4.1-R4.4, then establish a contract for rm before implementing its initial requirements. I'm also trying to pin down the exact SRD file naming convention and task title format from the project's existing patterns. I'm realizing the weights are stored in requirements.yaml, though I'm not certain of the exact path—it could be at the root or under docs/specs. Without seeing the file directly, I'll assume each R-item has a default weight of 1 since they're grouped in sets of four. For the mv tasks, the stitch agent will need the SRD file, the existing main.go implementation to modify, and the test file, while the rm contract requires its own SRD and the project's CLAUDE.md for reference patterns.\n\nFor rm implementation, I need the SRD, the contract file in cmd/rm/main.go, and the SIGPIPE handler from pkg/sys/. Looking at the test approach, the project uses differential testing with testutils.RunDiffTests, and I'm noticing the recent commits combine implementation and tests in single stitch tasks—but P2 says to split them. Let me check if that constraint actually applies here or if there's an exception for stitch tasks.\n\nActually, reviewing the git history more carefully, tasks 5396 and 5397 each handled 4 R-items with both implementation and tests included. The pattern seems to be that each R-item defines both the requirement and its test criteria, so the stitch agent implements the code and writes differential tests together. That's the established workflow here, so I'll follow it: 3-4 R-items per task with implementation and tests combined.\n\nNow I'm setting up the first task to handle R3.1 through R3.3 from the SRD, which means modifying the existing mv implementation and adding corresponding test cases. I'm checking the constraint about SRD requirement mapping—three task requirements for three SRD requirements is correct. Now I need to verify the granularity constraint: code tasks should target 5-8 requirements, 5-8 acceptance criteria, and 3-5 design decisions. I have 3 requirements, which is below the target range, so I need to reconsider the scope.\n\nBut wait, the hard constraints override the granularity guideline—tasks are limited to at most 4 SRD sub-requirements with a total weight not exceeding 4. Since the R3 group has exactly 3 items and R4 has 4, I should keep them separated to avoid crossing group boundaries.\n\nI'm also realizing that just referencing the SRD requirement ID won't be enough for the stitch agent—I need to include the actual requirement text in the task description so the agent understands what needs to be implemented without having to look it up separately.\n\nFrom the roadmap, I know mv is about moving or renaming files within and across filesystems, tied to use case rel07.1-uc002-mv. The stitch agent will still need to read the SRD for full context, but I should provide enough detail in the task description to guide implementation effectively.\n\nNow I need to finalize the 4 tasks while keeping in mind the contract task needs the \"(contract)\" suffix and a dependency of -1, with implementation tasks depending on that contract task.\n\nFor the mv tasks, since the package already exists and both tasks modify the same files (main.go and mv_test.go), I should make them sequential — Task 1 depends on Task 0 — to avoid conflicts.\n\nThis gives me a dependency structure where tasks 0 and 2 can run in parallel across different packages, then tasks 1 and 3 proceed sequentially after their prerequisites. Now I need to verify the weight budget while writing out the full task list. I need to make sure the task descriptions include the key project patterns from CLAUDE.md so the stitch agent understands the implementation approach — things like using the test utilities for differential testing, handling the reference binary lookup, installing the signal handler at startup, and the build command structure. These patterns should be referenced in the design decisions rather than fully repeated since the agent will read the full documentation anyway. Now I'm thinking through what the contract task for the rm component should actually contain. I'm reconsidering whether cmd/rm really needs a contract task since it's just package main with no exported types or interfaces—the only signature is func main(). But the constraint is explicit: I need a contract before implementation tasks unless the package already exists. I'll keep it lightweight by having the contract set up basic flag parsing and the test skeleton structure.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the file actions: the contract task creates both cmd/rm/main.go and cmd/rm/rm_test.go from scratch, while any implementation task that follows would modify those same files. Let me map out the full task sequence with this in mind. I'm noticing the commit message pattern in the repository — they follow the format \"[stitch] cmd/[name]: srd[number] R[range]\" — so my task titles should match that convention. Let me finalize the YAML structure with the correct naming pattern for all three tasks.\n\nLooking at the orchestrator logs, I can see a stitch run completed recently and a measure run just started, which confirms the pipeline is actively managing task states. I need to verify whether the SRD files follow the naming pattern `srd[NNN].yaml` or `srd[NNN